---
import PersonalVault from "@/layouts/PersonalVault.astro";
---

<PersonalVault
	title="Privacy policy"
	description="How Personal Vault accesses, uses, stores, and shares Google account data, and how access and saved copies can be removed."
>
	<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
	<p>
		I am Henrique Bastos, the operator of Personal Vault. This policy describes how I handle
		information through this private application.
	</p>

	<h2>1 Information accessed</h2>
	<p>
		With my authorization, Personal Vault can read my Google account identity, Calendar list and
		events, Gmail messages, and Google Docs.
	</p>
	<p>
		The import process selects meeting events, notification messages, document links, Gemini notes,
		and full transcripts when available. Those records may contain names, email addresses, dates,
		attendance details, and what participants said.
	</p>
	<p>
		Google's read-only permissions are broader than the selected meetings. The application does not
		request write access to email, Calendar events, or Google Docs.
	</p>

	<h2>2 Purpose and use</h2>
	<p>
		I use the imported information to maintain a searchable personal meeting archive, keep source
		references, and review discussions, decisions, and action items.
	</p>
	<p>
		I may request AI-assisted summaries and illustrated storyboards. I do not use imported Google
		data for advertising, sell it, or use it to develop, improve, or train generalized AI models.
	</p>
	<p>
		Personal Vault's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs will adhere to the <a
			href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
			>Google API Services User Data Policy</a
		>, including its Limited Use requirements.
	</p>

	<h2>3 Storage and protection</h2>
	<p>
		Meeting files, source snapshots, and derived documents are stored in my local Obsidian vault.
		Git history can preserve earlier versions, and Obsidian Sync can copy vault content to other
		devices and its service infrastructure.
	</p>
	<p>
		If I synchronize a Git repository or create backups, those destinations may also hold copies.
		Meeting content is not part of this public website.
	</p>
	<p>
		Google authorization credentials remain outside the vault. The local CLI stores encrypted
		account credentials separately and uses the macOS Keychain for its encryption key. Credentials
		are not included in meeting files or Git history.
	</p>

	<h2>4 Processing by other services</h2>
	<p>
		When I request AI processing, selected meeting content may be sent to the configured AI
		provider, such as OpenAI or Anthropic. This processing is not entirely local.
	</p>
	<p>
		Before sending content, I must have permission to process it and use provider settings and terms
		consistent with this policy. Providers may retain submitted content under their applicable
		service terms.
	</p>
	<p>
		Storage and synchronization providers process any copies I place with them under their
		applicable terms. I may also disclose information when required by law.
	</p>

	<h2>5 Retention and deletion</h2>
	<p>
		Imported files remain in the archive until I remove them. There is no automatic retention
		deadline.
	</p>
	<p>
		Deleting a current vault file does not necessarily remove its earlier versions from Git,
		synchronization history, backups, or AI service records. Those copies require separate deletion
		where the relevant service supports it.
	</p>
	<p>
		To ask about information concerning you or request its removal, contact <a
			href="mailto:henrique@bastos.net">henrique@bastos.net</a
		>. I will review the request and identify the relevant copies and any retention restrictions.
	</p>

	<h2>6 Revoking Google access</h2>
	<p>
		I can revoke the application's access through <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections"
			>Google account connections</a
		> and remove its local credentials. Revocation stops future authorized access but does not delete
		files already imported.
	</p>

	<h2>7 This website and policy changes</h2>
	<p>
		These application pages have no login form and do not load analytics scripts. The website host
		may process ordinary request information, such as IP addresses, to serve the pages.
	</p>
	<p>
		I will update this policy when the application's data practices change. The date below
		identifies the latest revision.
	</p>
</PersonalVault>
